    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The cave walls carry over 600 paintings and nearly 6,000 figures in all, animals, human figures and abstract signs, made over many generations; horses (364) and stags (90) are the commonest animals identified, and a single human figure appears at the bottom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Well</a>.</li><li>Four teenagers entered it on 12 September 1940 after the dog Robot investigated a hole left by an uprooted tree; it opened to visitors in 1948 and closed in 1963 after the breath and heat of 1,200 visitors a day damaged the paintings.</li><li>Interpretation is still debated, from records of hunting success and hunting ritual to trance visions and a structured arrangement of species and signs, and replicas now carry the paintings to the public while moulds are fought in the original.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The cave contains no images of reindeer apart from one in the Apse, although reindeer were the principal source of food for the artists, and no image of fish; the paintings show no landscape or vegetation either.</p></aside>
    </section>

